"<strong>\"The alpha hits the nucleus and bounces off.\"</strong> For gold at these energies it never touches it: it turns around at least {#} fm away, about four nuclear radii, pushed only by the Coulomb force.",
"<strong>\"Most alphas bounce back.\"</strong> Almost all pass with a small deflection; only about one in tens of thousands is turned past {#}° in a real foil. The surprise was that any came back at all.",
"<strong>\"The plum pudding atom fails because it has the wrong total charge.\"</strong> The total charge is the same in both models. What differs is how concentrated it is, which sets how strong the force can get.",
"<strong>\"A negative particle would be pulled straight into the nucleus.\"</strong> Only head on. With any impact parameter it has angular momentum, so it swings around the nucleus on a hyperbola and flies off again, turned by the same angle a positive particle would be.",
"<strong>\"The alpha follows a straight line and then turns sharply.\"</strong> The path is a smooth hyperbola, symmetric about the line through the point of closest approach.",
"Each alpha follows the exact Coulomb (hyperbolic) orbit, θ = {#} arctan(D/2b), with the closest approach r_min = (D/{#})({#} + √({#} + (2b/D)²)). Nonrelativistic mechanics is fine: v/c is at most about {#}.",
"The beam fires alphas at random b with uniform density over the beam's cross section. Counts are binned per steradian and compared with the Rutherford cross section, dσ/dΩ = (D/{#})² / sin⁴(θ/{#}).",
"The plum pudding atom is a uniform sphere of positive charge with point electrons ignored; the alpha takes a small random kick from each atom it crosses, and the kicks add like a random walk.",
"An option lets the nucleus recoil (lab frame); with it off, the nucleus is fixed.",
"The Projectile control swaps the alpha for an antialpha, the antihelium {#} nucleus: the same mass, charge −2e. The orbit is then the hyperbola branch around the nucleus, with r_min = (|D|/{#})({#}/sin(θ/{#}) − {#}) instead of (|D|/{#})({#}/sin(θ/{#}) + {#}). Antialphas are real but have never been made as a beam, so this is a thought experiment; a real one reaching the nucleus would annihilate, which the demo only flags. A negative ion would not work as a negative probe: its extra electron is bound by about an electron volt and is stripped in the first atomic layers of the foil.",
"The aluminum preset marks where the strong force would act (the alpha reaching the nuclear radius {#} A^⅓ fm). The demo does not model nuclear forces; it shows where the Rutherford formula stops applying.",
"Ask students to estimate the fraction of alphas turned past {#}° by a foil of thickness t from n t π b₉₀², then compare with the rail's readout.",
"Historical note: Geiger and Marsden's {#} result (a few alphas reflected from a metal foil) came two years before Rutherford's {#} explanation; their {#} paper tested the {#}/sin⁴(θ/{#}) law in detail."
